Subject: the object payload is opaque; its readers call the runtime

A zeo heap VALUE is a handle whose first two words are a real struct RBasic.
That much is MRI's layout, so RBASIC_CLASS, RB_FL_TEST_RAW, RB_BUILTIN_TYPE,
RB_TYPE_P, OBJ_FROZEN and RTYPEDDATA_P need no patch at all, and an extension
that sets FL_USER3 writes the word that owns it.

Behind those two words there is no struct RString and no struct RArray. So:

  - Every payload struct is declared and never defined -- RString, RArray,
    RRegexp, RObject, RMatch, RFile, RData, RTypedData. Upstream already
    declares RClass this way, and RStruct, RHash and RBignum were never
    defined in a public header. A `RSTRING(v)->len` is now a compile error
    naming the line, rather than a byte that means nothing.
  - Every macro that read one of those structs calls ruby/internal/zeo.h
    instead: RSTRING_PTR/LEN/END, RARRAY_LEN/AREF/ASET/PTR/CONST_PTR,
    DATA_PTR, RTYPEDDATA_DATA/GET_DATA/TYPE, RREGEXP_SRC/PTR, RMATCH_REGS.
  - DATA_PTR, RTYPEDDATA_DATA and RREGEXP_PTR are assigned through in real
    gems, so those three hand back a slot rather than a value.
  - ROBJECT_FIELDS raises. There is no ivar array to hand out.
  - RTYPEDDATA_EMBEDDED_P is false: zeo never embeds the payload.
  - Upstream's static assert that RData::data and RTypedData::data share an
    offset goes with the structs; neither carries the slot now.

Not patched, deliberately: rstruct.h, rhash.h and rclass.h were already all
function calls; rbignum.h reads only flags; RARRAY_PTR_USE keeps upstream's
rb_ary_ptr_use_start/_end pair.

